7.3.m:
Protect my wealth, O praiseworthy one;
guard it for me.
My first name, O Jātavedas, which mother and father gave [me] of old—
guard that until my return; we will bear your name.
Protect my offspring, O manly one;
guard it for me.
Guard these houses for me, you two, O Mitra and Varuṇa;
undestroyed, uninjured, may Pūṣan protect them,
until our return.
Protect my food, O Purīṣya;
guard it for me.
You have guarded my wealth, O praiseworthy one;
give it back to me.
O Agni of a thousand eyes, hundred heads, hundred radiances,
hundred are your in-breaths, a thousand your out-breaths.
You rule over thousandfold wealth;
give us of that;
may we be sharers of that from you.
Both my name and yours, O Jātavedas, like two garments changing, we wander;
both of us bearing them for skill and for life, each into our proper bodies, O Jātavedas.
You have guarded my offspring, O manly one;
give it back to me.
O Agni, lord of the house, may I be a good lord of the house through you, the lord of the house;
may you be a good lord of the house through me, the lord of the house.
May our Gārhapatya [fire] shine unshared for a hundred winters for both of us.
You have guarded my food, O Purīṣya;
give it back to me.

7.4.m:
At the Agnihotra a Stoma is to be yoked.
For he at whose Agnihotra a Stoma is yoked becomes wealthier.
With "Approaching the sacrifice..."—
these creatures indeed are [approached];
[with] "upa" [the Stoma is] a six-by-six Stoma;
he yokes just these [creatures].
With "Following his ancient radiance..."—
the gods indeed are the ancient [radiance];
they are the six-by-six Stoma;
he yokes just them
for the heavenly world.
With "Closer and closer now, O generous one, more and more to you..."—
this [earth] indeed is "closer";
having gone to the heavenly world, he firmly establishes himself on this [earth].
Those creatures which are about to come into being, they are "closer";
he yokes just them.
With "Around you let the invincible chariot..."—
with this [verse] he encloses the yoked Stoma.
With "Agni is the head, the peak of heaven..."—
this [verse] is a pair, possessing seed, rich in cattle;
that Gāyatrī which is the first among the draught-poles, that one is this; with the Gāyatrī he has attended [it].
With "Both of you, Indra and Agni, to invoke..."—
Indra is the sun;
he enters Agni by night;
he praises both of them together;
that Triṣṭubh which is the first among the draught-poles, that one is this; with the Triṣṭubh he has attended [it].
With "This one here was first established by the establishers..."—
for Agni was first established here in this world;
that Jagatī which is the first among the draught-poles, that one is this; with the Jagatī he has attended [it].
With "This is your seasonal womb..."—
for this [earth] is the womb of Agni, Agni of the sun;
that Anuṣṭubh which is the first among the draught-poles, that one is this; with the Anuṣṭubh he has attended [it].
With "I have sung of Dadhikrāvan..."—
that cattle-granting form of Agni which is connected with Dadhikrāvan,
that very form of his he seizes;
cattle become attentive to him.
Agni indeed is cattle;
just as an ox grows old, as a horse, as a man, so does this established [fire] grow old;
he is to be attended with the verses of the establishment of the fires;
for these are his bodies;
with them he makes him young again;
when he attends with the meters,
with them he makes him young again;
as he was when first established, so does he become for him.
He attends [him];
this is his yoking;
this is his self-restraint;
this is his petitioning;
just as one, having fetched [offerings], pays homage to a superior, even so is that.

7.5.m:
In the first half of the month he should attend [Agni] with a verse addressed to Agni and Soma.
For that which belongs to the full moon is the share of Agni and Soma;
to both of them he commits him;
the two, not departing, guard him.
In the second half of the month [he should attend] with one addressed to Indra and Agni;
for that which belongs to the new moon is the share of Indra and Agni;
to both of them he commits him;
the two, not departing, guard him.
Agni and Soma commit him to the first half of the month;
Indra and Agni to the second half of the month.
Handing him over in succession, indeed, the gods, not departing, guard him who, knowing thus, attends Agni.
"For what purpose is Agni attended?" they ask.
That indeed is Agni's meal,
when one attends Agni;
for Agni this meal is prepared.
He attends in the evening, not in the morning;
therefore in the evening [food is served] for a guest against sin, and not so in the morning.
There is a benediction for sacrifice after sacrifice;
when one attends Agni,
he prays for this very benediction of the Agnihotra.
Prajāpati created creatures;
he created Agni first of all;
for him he ordained this share, namely the attendance on the fire.
When one attends Agni,
he enriches him with his own share.
Agni indeed longed for yoking, as a horse desires a chariot;
when one attends Agni,
he yokes him; he fulfills his desire.
He who fulfills the desire of a mortal becomes wealthier;
he prospers and becomes wealthier who, knowing thus, attends Agni.
With "upa..."
he unites with [creatures] by this [verse].
With "Agni is the head..."
he pours out seed by this [verse].
With "Both of you, Indra and Agni..."—
Indra and Agni are the in-breath and the out-breath;
he establishes in-breath and out-breath by this [verse].
With "This one here was first established..."
he deposits the embryo by this [verse].
With "Following his ancient radiance..."
he forms the udder by this [verse].
With "This is your seasonal womb..."
he both brings forth and causes to grow by this [verse].
Fitted indeed are the offspring born to him who, knowing thus, attends Agni.

7.12.m:
To the creator, the wise, the one to be known, a respectful word to the bull, the virile,
from whence fear comes, let there be freedom from fear for us; I sacrifice away the slights of the gods.
Attend upon Agni with firewood; awaken the guest with ghṛta;
offer the oblations into him.
O Agni, that flame of yours, offered with ghṛta, shines upward,
lapping at the mouth of the ladles.
Let my ghee-holding ladles go toward you, O beloved;
O Agni, enjoy our oblations.
O Agni, unite the offspring and the quarters together with the cattle;
place in him the realms that were in the impulse of Savitṛ.
I unite your heart; united let your mind be;
united let your bodies be; united let your breath of life be.
Those bodies of yours that are dear, united in affection let your hearts be;
let your soul be united in affection, united in affection my bodies.
The Aśvattha that, pregnant with the Śamī, grew upon you,
that of yours I take together with the sacrificial banners.
Into myself I take Agni first, together with the increase of wealth for luster here, O gods;
in myself I place offspring, in myself prosperity; may we rejoice for a hundred winters with good heroes.
That Agni of ours, O ancestors, who, immortal, entered mortals inside the hearts,
him we grasp around in our own self; let him not go away abandoning us;
let us not go away abandoning him.
O great one, mistress of the clan, seat of truth, facing hither come to this support of riches;
pregnant, generate the noble Jātavedas, who goes in front of the sacrifices.
A sweet wave has come up from the ocean, placing further onward for universal sovereignty;
both those liberal patrons and we, may we gather sweet refreshment and vigor together.
So much were you in the beginning;
when spreading out apart, O goddess,
driven by the gods you dispersed through greatness; you became firm with gravel in the third heaven;
you conquered the worlds, the four intermediate regions.
Rise up, O Agni, from Mother Earth; enter the clans from the great abode;
the pious, swift in the contest, placed you, the oblation-bearer, the guardian of the world.
Whatever of heaven or of earth has come into being from the two well-acting, generous ones,
on their back let Jātavedas sit, generating offspring and wealth for us.
Agni the gleaming, with gleaming chariot, whose course is golden, Vaiśvānara sitting in the waters, finding light,
penetrating, hasty, encompassed with powers, energetic, of fair splendor, the gods have placed here.
Whatever un-afflicted thing of the earth came into being along with you,
that let Agni give to Agni; let this one be placed upon that.
Whatever un-afflicted thing of the atmosphere, of heaven, came into being along with you,
that let Agni give to Agni; let this one be placed upon that.

8.3.m:
At eight paces [the fire] is to be established by a Brahmin.
The Gāyatrī has eight syllables;
the Brahmin has the Gāyatrī meter;
he establishes him in the proper place of his own meter.
Just as a father invigorates his son, even so his own meter invigorates him.
At eleven paces it is to be established by a Rājanya.
The Triṣṭubh has eleven syllables;
the Rājanya has the Triṣṭubh meter;
he establishes him in the proper place of his own meter.
Just as a father invigorates his son, even so his own meter invigorates him.
At twelve paces it is to be established by a Vaiśya.
The Jagatī has twelve syllables;
the Vaiśya has the Jagatī meter;
he establishes him in the proper place of his own meter.
Just as a father invigorates his son, even so his own meter invigorates him.
"May I obtain the unlimited," thus indeed he establishes the fire;
it is to be established without limit,
for the attainment of the unlimited.
As far as he might think with his eye, at that distance he should establish it;
the eye is truth;
he establishes him in truth.
It is to be established in the forenoon;
this is indeed the auspicious day;
he establishes him on an auspicious day.
At dawn, before the rising of the sun, it is to be established;
in this world Prajāpati created creatures;
they were procreated;
it is to be established thus for procreation.
Night belongs to Agni, day belongs to Indra;
if he should establish it when the sun has risen, he would depart from the color belonging to Agni;
if when it has not risen, from that belonging to Indra.
The latter is to be established when [the sun] has not risen, the former when it has risen;
he obtains both colors of Indra and Agni.
The night is Asuric by color, the day is bright.
He who establishes it when the sun has risen establishes it in the bright.
By night [it is] when it has not risen, by day when it has risen;
he who establishes it when the sun has risen establishes it by day.
Men do not quite know the lunar mansion, as it were;
for they deliberate, as it were; but indeed by the risen [sun] is the auspicious day;
he who establishes it when the sun has risen establishes it on an auspicious day.
"Seven are your kindling-sticks, O Agni, seven tongues, seven seers, seven dear domains;
knowing the seven Hotṛ-offices, fill the seven wombs with ghee."
As many as are the bodies of Agni,
he who, establishing him, leaves them thirsty, he becomes thirsty.
So many are the bodies of Agni, sixfold seven by seven;
those indeed of his he gratifies;
he does not become thirsty.
With a full ladle he offers to Prajāpati in thought;
Prajāpati is full;
he reaches Prajāpati.

8.7.m:
The oblation has been poured out, brought near, [and] remains unsprinkled.
Then, having sprinkled the central gaming-place and thrown down the dice, he offers [with the verse]: "Varuṇa of firm vow has sat down in the homesteads for universal sovereignty, he of good counsel."
Three times indeed did the Virāj stride forth: the Gārhapatya, the Āhavanīya, [and] the central gaming-place.
He makes him stride after the striding of the Virāj;
he stands firm;
he attains a firm foundation.
He offers for the upholding of order;
he makes him one who upholds order.
As Savitṛ the supporter, they slay a cow;
they play for it;
they present it to those sitting in the assembly;
by that, he is sought [and] gratified for him.
"Now indeed Bṛhaspati…"—thus he offers in the summoning place with a verse containing the word "mantra" addressed to the All-Gods;
he grasps the sacred formulation (mantra) for him;
coming together, they deliberate upon it.
Together indeed were the gods and men at the Odanapacana.
Those men encroached upon the gods.
Having held back food from them, the gods strode up to the Gārhapatya.
They followed them thither.
Those men indeed encroached upon the gods.
Having held back cattle from them, the gods strode up to the Āhavanīya.
They followed them thither.
Those men indeed encroached upon the gods.
Having held back the sacrifice from them, the gods strode up to the assembly (sabhā).
They followed them thither.
Those men indeed encroached upon the gods.
Having held back the Virāj from them, the gods strode up to the summoning place (āmantraṇa).
Thence they did not pursue them further.
These indeed are the transitions of the gods.
He who knows thus strides up to a better and better world.
This indeed is the unconquered truth of the gods, namely the summoning place.
Therefore, one should go to the summoning place early in the morning;
he goes to truth itself.
Therefore, one should not go to the summoning place uninvited;
therefore, one should not speak untruth in the summoning place.
Bearer (vahni) indeed is the name of the Odanapacana;
a bearer is born to him who knows thus.
The homestead is the Gārhapatya;
he becomes possessed of a homestead who knows thus.
The hearth is the Āhavanīya;
the sacrifice bows toward him who knows thus.
Far-extended is the central gaming-place;
he extends with offspring and cattle who knows thus.
Unattained is the summoning place;
he who seeks to reach him does not reach him who knows thus.

8.8.m:
A goat is to be given
for having the same origin.
A garment is to be given;
a garment belongs to all deities;
for the reaching of the deities.
An increasing sacrificial gift is to be given;
the sacrificer indeed increases after the increase of the sacrificial gift.
A sacrificial gift that brings near [milk] is to be given;
a milch-cow indeed is one that brings near;
for she brings near milk;
nothing becomes less for him who establishes the fire.
A milch-cow is to be given to the Hotṛ,
for the winning of blessings;
an ox to the Adhvaryu;
he wins the very bearer of the sacrifice.
A chariot is to be given;
a divine chariot indeed is yoked, namely the sacrifice;
by means of the human chariot he mounts the divine chariot.
A pair of cattle is to be given,
for the winning of a pair.
Six are to be given;
six indeed are the seasons;
he establishes himself in the seasons.
Twelve are to be given;
twelve months are the year;
for the attainment of the year.
If he should fall away beyond twelve, he should give twenty-four;
twenty-four syllables has the Gāyatrī;
the Gāyatrī is the mouth of the sacrifice;
he does not step beyond the Gāyatrī, the mouth of the sacrifice.
There are three oblations;
three are these worlds;
he attains these worlds.
Three times did the Virāj stride forth;
he makes him stride after the striding of the Virāj.
In that [there is one] to the purifying [Agni] (pavamāna),
by that he purifies him;
in that [there is one] to the cleanser [Agni] (pāvaka),
food indeed is the cleanser;
by that he bestows food upon him;
in that [there is one] to the bright [Agni] (śuci),
by that he makes him pure, fit for sacrifice, worthy of worship.
"May I be possessed of cattle, may I be possessed of offspring, may I be possessed of splendor"—thus indeed he establishes the fire.
Breath indeed is the purifying one;
from breath cattle are born;
in that it is for Agni Pavamāna,
by that he generates cattle for him.
Waters indeed are the cleansers;
from the waters offspring are born;
in that it is for Agni Pāvaka,
by that he generates offspring for him.
Yonder sun indeed is the bright one;
he is the bestower of splendor;
in that it is for Agni Śuci,
yonder sun bestows splendor upon him.
The gods and the asuras were in conflict.
As they were going into victory, that Agni deposited his bodies in three places: a third in cattle, a third in the waters, a third in yonder sun.
In that these oblations are poured out,
he gathers together those very bodies of his;
with his body intact, with his splendor intact, he is thus established.
These worlds indeed are these oblations.
They have separate sacrificial grasses;
he fills these worlds.
If they were to have separate sacrificial grasses,
he would not leave
space for generation;
offspring indeed are born to what is deficient.
The two latter [oblations] are to be performed on the same sacrificial grass;
he made space for generation;
upon it he is born.
We know this world, we know that [intermediate world]; yonder [highest world] we either see or do not see;
therefore the two latter [oblations] are to be performed on the same sacrificial grass.
Cattle indeed are these oblations,
the sacrificial cakes are clarified butter,
Agni is Rudra.
If he were to pour out immediately after, he would consign the cattle to Rudra; he would become without cattle.
It is to be poured out after a year;
by means of the year he appeases him.
Loose indeed and of different origin is made of the sacrifice when he pours out after a year;
it is to be poured out immediately after,
for firmness, for having the same origin.

8.12.m:
They deliberate: "Is the fire to be established or not to be established by one who is about to establish the fire on the morrow?"
It is indeed to be established.
For without sacrifice is he who is without fire.
A tied goat should stay through that night.
Of the splendor of Agni is the goat;
it is that very Agni that he establishes.
It should be spotted;
for it belongs to all deities.
He who is about to establish the fire on the morrow should observe a vow that night;
he should not eat meat;
he should not approach a woman.
Every man has fire [within him];
Agni would depart from him.
For this is his womb;
from this he is produced, [namely] he who brings him out.
The right [fire] that is churned, is churned for brahma-splendor alone.
Moreover, he makes him having the same origin with the [two] fires.
Yet it is difficult to keep;
they should churn his day by day,
or he should bring it from wherever [he can].
This indeed is his un-won body;
that very [body] he wins.
Where he sees [fire] blazing in the distance, from there he should bring it;
these deities indeed become manifest;
them indeed he wins.
He should bring it from a roasting-pan for one who desires food;
this indeed is his food-eating body;
that very [body] he wins.
From the house of a Brahmin or a Vaiśya who is nourished like an Asura, he should bring it;
whatever that nourishment is, whatever food, that he wins.
In his house, however, he should not eat from it.
Three times he raises [it] up;
three are these worlds;
these worlds indeed he wins.
In that he raises [it] up,
this world he wins thereby.
In that he runs forward toward the east,
the atmosphere [he wins] thereby.
In that he establishes [it],
yonder [world he wins] thereby.
Having established it, he should not lift it up again;
that is a departure;
he becomes worse off.
From the life-breaths indeed Prajāpati created creatures;
they were generated.
He should lift it up by that measure,
for generation.
He should not lift it up too high;
Agni would burn up his life-breaths; he would perish.
He should not place it down low;
that is a departure;
he becomes worse off.

8.13.m:
Eaters of what is not sacrificed were his gods formerly;
now he turns toward eaters of what is sacrificed.
They depart with his vigor;
he wins vigor indeed;
he makes them partakers of a share.
They sacrifice in yonder [fire], they offer in this one; they cook in yonder [fire],
for the uninterruptedness of this.
Moreover, he established him in being Vaiśvānara.
Those indeed who sacrificed with the sacrifice prospered.
Men do not quite know the sacrifice;
therefore not everyone prospers.
Formerly, indeed, they used to sacrifice with the New and Full Moon sacrifices accompanied by sacrificial gifts.
This boiled rice that is cooked,
he gives it as this sacrificial gift,
for the prosperity of the sacrifice.
He has performed a sacrifice by that which he sacrifices;
by this boiled rice that is cooked, he has performed a pious work;
he indeed is one who has performed sacrifice and pious works who cooks this.
Prajāpati indeed assigned portions to the gods.
He thought: "I have excluded myself from the sacrifice."
He saw this boiled rice;
he prepared it as a portion for himself.
Prajāpati's portion indeed is this.
He should cook a great, boundless [portion];
boundless is Prajāpati;
he wins Prajāpati indeed.
The gods and the Asuras contended.
Those gods sacrificed toward Prajāpati;
the Asuras offered into one another's mouth.
Those gods cooked this boiled rice;
they poured it out as a portion for Prajāpati.
Seeing that portion,
Prajāpati turned toward the gods.
Thereupon the gods prospered, the Asuras were defeated.
He who, knowing thus, brings the Anvāhārya [rice] prospers himself; his rival is defeated.
Some gods are to be praised, others are to be served.
The gods are to be praised, the Brahmins are to be served.
By the sacrifice he pleases those to be praised; by the Anvāhārya those to be served.
Both become pleased at his sacrifice.
"Prajāpati's portion you are, full of vigor, full of milk;
undecaying you are, for undecayingness [I accept] you;
Undecaying by name you are;
do not decay for me;
protect my in-breath and out-breath;
protect my equalized breath and diffused breath;
protect my upward breath and form;
vigor you are;
place vigor in me;
come unto me!"
With these words,
he prospers Prajāpati directly.
